<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Adaptive icon for API 26+ (Android 8.0).

    The launcher's mask is a system-drawn shape that varies by OEM and by the
    user's chosen icon shape, so neither layer carries a rounded corner or a
    shadow — those are the launcher's job. The background is therefore a flat
    fill and all the artwork lives in the foreground (**D-66**, **D-67**).

    The foreground references @drawable/ic_launcher_foreground, not
    @mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground. flutter_launcher_icons writes the adaptive
    foreground into res/drawable-* and wraps it in this <inset>; the mipmap-*
    copy is a separate legacy artefact. Pointing at the mipmap name here
    resolves to a file this tool does not regenerate, which is how the round
    variant (ic_launcher_round.xml) kept serving a stale icon after the source
    artwork changed.

    The <inset> is 0% because pubspec.yaml sets
    adaptive_icon_foreground_inset: 0, keeping the two in step.

    There is deliberately no <monochrome> layer. Themed-icon support wants a
    single-colour silhouette, and this artwork is a multi-colour mark whose
    meaning comes from the grey/orange split. Pointing <monochrome> at the
    colour foreground would just have the system tint the whole bitmap one
    colour, producing indistinct blobs — an icon that looks broken on exactly
    the themed-icon surfaces the layer exists for. Omitting the layer means
    Android falls back to the colour adaptive icon, which is correct. If a
    monochrome silhouette is ever wanted it needs its own drawn asset.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground>
        <inset
            android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground"
            android:inset="0%" />
    </foreground>
</adaptive-icon>
